---
// Lesson 5.2 — The Door: Breaking In with Events
// Lever.Interact, the E key + distance check, UnityEvents in the Inspector.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>5.2 — The Door: Breaking In with Events</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You have an axe; the cave door does not care — yet. Today you wire
    the last gap shut: press E near a lever, the lever fires a
    <strong>UnityEvent</strong>, and the door opens without the lever
    ever knowing what a door is. That gap between cause and effect is
    called <em>abstraction</em>, and it is the most AP-exam-flavored
    idea in the whole starter.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Read the player half of the interaction — PlayerController.Update:</p>
    <pre><code>if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.E))
{
    Lever[] levers = FindObjectsByType&lt;Lever&gt;(FindObjectsSortMode.None);
    foreach (Lever lever in levers)
    {
        if (Vector3.Distance(transform.position, lever.transform.position) &lt; 2.0f)
        {
            lever.Interact();
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>And the lever's whole personality — Lever.cs:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private UnityEvent onLeverOn;
[SerializeField] private UnityEvent onLeverOff;

public void Interact()
{
    isOn = !isOn; // Toggle state
    if (isOn)
    {
        if (spriteRenderer != null &amp;&amp; onSprite != null) spriteRenderer.sprite = onSprite;
        onLeverOn.Invoke();
    }
    else
    {
        if (spriteRenderer != null &amp;&amp; offSprite != null) spriteRenderer.sprite = offSprite;
        onLeverOff.Invoke();
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Predict: the word "door" appears nowhere in Lever.cs. How can a
        lever open a door it has never met? Which window would you open
        to teach the lever about the door — and what does that say
        about where the "game rules" actually live?
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The AP exam calls a procedure that calls other procedures
            without knowing their names <strong>abstraction</strong> —
            the same word as the Create PT's "manage complexity." What
            complexity does UnityEvent manage here — write down what
            the lever WOULD have needed to know otherwise.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the 2-unit social contract.</strong> Press E
    anywhere: the code finds EVERY lever and asks each one "within
    2.0 units of me?" — the same Distance pattern as the turret
    (<a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>), pointed inward instead of outward.
    Stand near the cave door's lever and press E: sprite swaps
    (onSprite), isOn flips, and the UnityEvent fires. Press E again:
    everything un-fires. Toggles are honest machines — every cause
    has a reverse.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — wire the door, no code.</strong> Select the
    Switch/lever object near the cave entrance. In the Inspector find
    <strong>On Lever On</strong> (the UnityEvent). The plus button
    adds a listener: drag the DOOR GameObject into the slot, choose
    what to call on it — <code>GameObject → SetActive(false)</code>
    for a vanishing door, or destroy it, or play an animation. Run:
    press E near it. The door obeys. You just changed the game's
    rules from the Inspector, with zero lines of code.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — respect the gate.</strong> Here is the design
    contract the next lesson honors: the door should only open for
    the axe-holder. Does the lever check hasAxe? No — the lever
    checks nothing. Where SHOULD the check live: the lever, the door,
    or the player? Write your answer; tomorrow's cave lesson
    (<a href="5.3.html">5.3</a>) takes the door off its hinges
    anyway, so argue it as a designer would.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The event that wouldn't</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Walk away 5 units and press E. What does the
                foreach-_distance combo protect you from — and what
                would pressing E do EVERYWHERE without it?</li>
            <li>Wire the door's SetActive(false) into
                <strong>On Lever Off</strong> instead of On. Now what
                is the E-press sequence to get in — and is that a
                puzzle or a bug?</li>
            <li>Leave the lever's onSprite field empty. Does the event
                still fire? What does that tell you about the sprite
                swap and the event — which is decoration and which is
                the machinery?</li>
            <li>Add a SECOND lever and stand between them (both within
                2 units). Press E once — what happened, and should
                one keypress ever flip two switches?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Event design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Wire the lever to TWO listeners: open the door AND play
                a sound/animation. Does the lever notice? Why is
                "whoever wants to know, listens" the scalable
                direction?</li>
            <li>Make a FALSE wall: the lever opens a secret passage
                instead of the cave. How many lines of code did that
                cost?</li>
            <li>Hard: make the lever only work when hasAxe is true.
                Which script gains an if — and can you do it with
                zero changes to Lever.cs?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain UnityEvents as cause-and-effect with a gap: the lever never names the door</li>
            <li>Wire a listener in the Inspector and change game rules with no code</li>
            <li>Explain the E-key interaction: FindObjectsByType + Distance &lt; 2.0</li>
            <li>Argue where an item-gate check belongs</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-05/index.html">Unit 5: The Axe &amp; The Cave</a> — levers, events, scene switches</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — FindObjectsByType, UnityEvents</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="5.3.html">5.3 — Into the Cave: Scene Loading &amp; Mode Switch</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="5.2" title="5.2a The Door: Breaking In with Events — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
